{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query (FKA React Query) is often described as the missing data-fetching library for web applications, but in more technical terms, it makes "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fetching, caching, synchronizing and updating server state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in your web applications a breeze."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"motivation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Motivation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most core web frameworks "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"do not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" come with an opinionated way of fetching or updating data in a holistic way. Because of this developers end up building either meta-frameworks which encapsulate strict opinions about data-fetching, or they invent their own ways of fetching data. This usually means cobbling together component-based state and side-effects, or using more general purpose state management libraries to store and provide asynchronous data throughout their apps."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While most traditional state management libraries are great for working with client state, they are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not so great at working with async or server state"}]},{"type":"text","value":". This is because "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server state is totally different"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For starters, server state:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Is persisted remotely in a location you do not control or own"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requires asynchronous APIs for fetching and updating"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implies shared ownership and can be changed by other people without your knowledge"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Can potentially become \"out of date\" in your applications if you're not careful"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once you grasp the nature of server state in your application, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"even more challenges will arise"}]},{"type":"text","value":" as you go, for example:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Caching... (possibly the hardest thing to do in programming)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deduping multiple requests for the same data into a single request"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Updating \"out of date\" data in the background"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Knowing when data is \"out of date\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reflecting updates to data as quickly as possible"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance optimizations like pagination and lazy loading data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managing memory and garbage collection of server state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memoizing query results with structural sharing"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you're not overwhelmed by that list, then that must mean that you've probably solved all of your server state problems already and deserve an award. However, if you are like a vast majority of people, you either have yet to tackle all or most of these challenges and we're only scratching the surface!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query is hands down one of the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"best"}]},{"type":"text","value":" libraries for managing server state. It works amazingly well "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"out-of-the-box, with zero-config, and can be customized"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to your liking as your application grows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query allows you to defeat and overcome the tricky challenges and hurdles of "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and control your app data before it starts to control you."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a more technical note, React Query will likely:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Help you remove "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"many"}]},{"type":"text","value":" lines of complicated and misunderstood code from your application and replace with just a handful of lines of React Query logic."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Make your application more maintainable and easier to build new features without worrying about wiring up new server state data sources"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Have a direct impact on your end-users by making your application feel faster and more responsive than ever before."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Potentially help you save on bandwidth and increase memory performance"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"enough-talk-show-me-some-code-already","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enough talk, show me some code already!"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the example below, you can see React Query in its most basic and simple form being used to fetch the GitHub stats for the React Query GitHub project itself:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://codesandbox.io/s/github/tannerlinsley/react-query/tree/main/examples/react/simple","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open in CodeSandbox"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  QueryClient,\n  QueryClientProvider,\n  useQuery,\n} from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Example />\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction Example() {\n  const { isLoading, error, data } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['repoData'],\n    queryFn: () =>\n      fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/TanStack/query').then((res) =>\n        res.json(),\n      ),\n  })\n\n  if (isLoading) return 'Loading...'\n\n  if (error) return 'An error has occurred: ' + error.message\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>{data.name}</h1>\n      <p>{data.description}</p>\n      <strong>👀 {data.subscribers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>✨ {data.stargazers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>🍴 {data.forks_count}</strong>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"you-talked-me-into-it-so-what-now","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You talked me into it, so what now?"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider taking the official "},{"type":"link","href":"https://ui.dev/react-query?from=tanstack","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Query Course"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (or buying it for your whole team!)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learn React Query at your own pace with our amazingly thorough "},{"type":"link","href":"../installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Walkthrough Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/useQuery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API Reference"}]}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"motivation","text":"Motivation","level":2},{"id":"enough-talk-show-me-some-code-already","text":"Enough talk, show me some code already!","level":2},{"id":"you-talked-me-into-it-so-what-now","text":"You talked me into it, so what now?","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Query (FKA React Query) is often described as the missing data-fetching library for web applications, but in more technical terms, it makes **fetching, caching, synchronizing and updating server state** in your web applications a breeze.\n\n## Motivation\n\nMost core web frameworks **do not** come with an opinionated way of fetching or updating data in a holistic way. Because of this developers end up building either meta-frameworks which encapsulate strict opinions about data-fetching, or they invent their own ways of fetching data. This usually means cobbling together component-based state and side-effects, or using more general purpose state management libraries to store and provide asynchronous data throughout their apps.\n\nWhile most traditional state management libraries are great for working with client state, they are **not so great at working with async or server state**. This is because **server state is totally different**. For starters, server state:\n\n- Is persisted remotely in a location you do not control or own\n- Requires asynchronous APIs for fetching and updating\n- Implies shared ownership and can be changed by other people without your knowledge\n- Can potentially become \"out of date\" in your applications if you're not careful\n\nOnce you grasp the nature of server state in your application, **even more challenges will arise** as you go, for example:\n\n- Caching... (possibly the hardest thing to do in programming)\n- Deduping multiple requests for the same data into a single request\n- Updating \"out of date\" data in the background\n- Knowing when data is \"out of date\"\n- Reflecting updates to data as quickly as possible\n- Performance optimizations like pagination and lazy loading data\n- Managing memory and garbage collection of server state\n- Memoizing query results with structural sharing\n\nIf you're not overwhelmed by that list, then that must mean that you've probably solved all of your server state problems already and deserve an award. However, if you are like a vast majority of people, you either have yet to tackle all or most of these challenges and we're only scratching the surface!\n\nReact Query is hands down one of the _best_ libraries for managing server state. It works amazingly well **out-of-the-box, with zero-config, and can be customized** to your liking as your application grows.\n\nReact Query allows you to defeat and overcome the tricky challenges and hurdles of _server state_ and control your app data before it starts to control you.\n\nOn a more technical note, React Query will likely:\n\n- Help you remove **many** lines of complicated and misunderstood code from your application and replace with just a handful of lines of React Query logic.\n- Make your application more maintainable and easier to build new features without worrying about wiring up new server state data sources\n- Have a direct impact on your end-users by making your application feel faster and more responsive than ever before.\n- Potentially help you save on bandwidth and increase memory performance\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\n## Enough talk, show me some code already!\n\nIn the example below, you can see React Query in its most basic and simple form being used to fetch the GitHub stats for the React Query GitHub project itself:\n\n[Open in CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/s/github/tannerlinsley/react-query/tree/main/examples/react/simple)\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  QueryClient,\n  QueryClientProvider,\n  useQuery,\n} from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Example />\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction Example() {\n  const { isLoading, error, data } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['repoData'],\n    queryFn: () =>\n      fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/TanStack/query').then((res) =>\n        res.json(),\n      ),\n  })\n\n  if (isLoading) return 'Loading...'\n\n  if (error) return 'An error has occurred: ' + error.message\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>{data.name}</h1>\n      <p>{data.description}</p>\n      <strong>👀 {data.subscribers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>✨ {data.stargazers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>🍴 {data.forks_count}</strong>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## You talked me into it, so what now?\n\n- Consider taking the official [React Query Course](https://ui.dev/react-query?from=tanstack) (or buying it for your whole team!)\n- Learn React Query at your own pace with our amazingly thorough [Walkthrough Guide](../installation) and [API Reference](../reference/useQuery)\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: overview\ntitle: Overview\n---\n\nTanStack Query (FKA React Query) is often described as the missing data-fetching library for web applications, but in more technical terms, it makes **fetching, caching, synchronizing and updating server state** in your web applications a breeze.\n\n## Motivation\n\nMost core web frameworks **do not** come with an opinionated way of fetching or updating data in a holistic way. Because of this developers end up building either meta-frameworks which encapsulate strict opinions about data-fetching, or they invent their own ways of fetching data. This usually means cobbling together component-based state and side-effects, or using more general purpose state management libraries to store and provide asynchronous data throughout their apps.\n\nWhile most traditional state management libraries are great for working with client state, they are **not so great at working with async or server state**. This is because **server state is totally different**. For starters, server state:\n\n- Is persisted remotely in a location you do not control or own\n- Requires asynchronous APIs for fetching and updating\n- Implies shared ownership and can be changed by other people without your knowledge\n- Can potentially become \"out of date\" in your applications if you're not careful\n\nOnce you grasp the nature of server state in your application, **even more challenges will arise** as you go, for example:\n\n- Caching... (possibly the hardest thing to do in programming)\n- Deduping multiple requests for the same data into a single request\n- Updating \"out of date\" data in the background\n- Knowing when data is \"out of date\"\n- Reflecting updates to data as quickly as possible\n- Performance optimizations like pagination and lazy loading data\n- Managing memory and garbage collection of server state\n- Memoizing query results with structural sharing\n\nIf you're not overwhelmed by that list, then that must mean that you've probably solved all of your server state problems already and deserve an award. However, if you are like a vast majority of people, you either have yet to tackle all or most of these challenges and we're only scratching the surface!\n\nReact Query is hands down one of the _best_ libraries for managing server state. It works amazingly well **out-of-the-box, with zero-config, and can be customized** to your liking as your application grows.\n\nReact Query allows you to defeat and overcome the tricky challenges and hurdles of _server state_ and control your app data before it starts to control you.\n\nOn a more technical note, React Query will likely:\n\n- Help you remove **many** lines of complicated and misunderstood code from your application and replace with just a handful of lines of React Query logic.\n- Make your application more maintainable and easier to build new features without worrying about wiring up new server state data sources\n- Have a direct impact on your end-users by making your application feel faster and more responsive than ever before.\n- Potentially help you save on bandwidth and increase memory performance\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n\n## Enough talk, show me some code already!\n\nIn the example below, you can see React Query in its most basic and simple form being used to fetch the GitHub stats for the React Query GitHub project itself:\n\n[Open in CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/s/github/tannerlinsley/react-query/tree/main/examples/react/simple)\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  QueryClient,\n  QueryClientProvider,\n  useQuery,\n} from '@tanstack/react-query'\n\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient()\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>\n      <Example />\n    </QueryClientProvider>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction Example() {\n  const { isLoading, error, data } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['repoData'],\n    queryFn: () =>\n      fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/TanStack/query').then((res) =>\n        res.json(),\n      ),\n  })\n\n  if (isLoading) return 'Loading...'\n\n  if (error) return 'An error has occurred: ' + error.message\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h1>{data.name}</h1>\n      <p>{data.description}</p>\n      <strong>👀 {data.subscribers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>✨ {data.stargazers_count}</strong>{' '}\n      <strong>🍴 {data.forks_count}</strong>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n[//]: # 'Example'\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n\n## You talked me into it, so what now?\n\n- Consider taking the official [React Query Course](https://ui.dev/react-query?from=tanstack) (or buying it for your whole team!)\n- Learn React Query at your own pace with our amazingly thorough [Walkthrough Guide](../installation) and [API Reference](../reference/useQuery)\n\n[//]: # 'Materials'\n"}
